\section{How the random effects enter (\code{groups})}{

A mixed model predicts different things for the same row, depending on the
question asked. So \code{groups} has no default for a model with random effects
or a correlation over time; it must be named. The choices:
\itemize{
\item \code{"fitted"}: the unit the row names, with that unit's own estimated effects.
A level the fit has not seen is predicted as a new unit, and marked in the
\code{new_level} column.
\item \code{"new"}: a new, unseen unit. Its effects are drawn from the fitted
distribution of effects, afresh in every parameter draw. Rows naming the
same unit share its effects within a draw.
\item \code{"typical"}: a typical unit, with its effects at zero. For a nonlinear
link this is not the average unit.
\item \code{"population"}: the average over units. For \code{target = "mean"}, the mean is
averaged over the distribution of effects, as illume's
\code{predict(groups = "population")} does. For a new observation, a randomly chosen unit's outcome is the same
thing as a new unit's, so it equals \code{"new"}.
}

Give one choice for every term, or a list per term, as in
\code{list(site = "fitted", ar = "new")}. The correlation over time is the term
\code{"ar"}. Smooth terms are part of the mean structure and always enter as
fitted.
}

\section{Over time}{

With a correlation over time (\code{ilm_ar1()}, \code{ilm_car1()} or \code{ilm_rw1()}), a
seen series under \code{"fitted"} is continued from its fitted cells:
\itemize{
\item \strong{after its last cell}, a forecast carried forward from that cell;
\item \strong{between two cells}, drawn from the bridge between them;
\item \strong{before its first cell}, carried backward from it.
}

A new series, or any series under \code{"new"}, is a fresh path. AR(1) and CAR(1)
paths start from their stationary distribution, and a random walk starts
from zero at the series' first time. Each series' new times are drawn in
sequence, each given the one before, so a draw is one path through them,
and rows naming the same series and time share one value, across scenarios
too. The model's own parameters are used throughout; nothing is refitted.

For a mean or the linear predictor, the estimate at a seen series' new time
is the point forecast: the fitted cells carried forward with no new shocks,
at the estimates, as a Kalman filter's point forecast is. For a new series,
as for a new unit, it is the predictive median. \code{as.data.frame()} gives the
expected value beside it, as \code{mean}; for counts it can be well above the
estimate at long horizons (see \code{\link[=as.data.frame.ilm_pred]{as.data.frame.ilm_pred()}}).
}

\section{Time and series columns}{

A prediction records which of its columns hold time and the series, for the
trend check (see \emph{Checks}) and for scaled errors read from the history
(\code{\link[=ilm_scores]{ilm_scores()}}'s \verb{history =}). It takes them from, in turn:
\itemize{
\item a correlation over time built by name, such as \code{ilm_ar1(~ week | region)}:
its own columns. Naming a different column in \code{time} or \code{group} is an
error, since the model can use only its own, and so are rows from
\code{\link[=ilm_future]{ilm_future()}} built around a different column that leave the model's own
missing;
\item \code{time} and \code{group}, which name columns of \code{newdata};
\item the rows \code{\link[=ilm_future]{ilm_future()}} built, which record their columns.
}

A model with time only in its formula, such as a trend or a random slope in
time, has no correlation over time to say. Rows from \code{\link[=ilm_future]{ilm_future()}} say for
it; rows made otherwise, or changed by \code{merge()} or \code{subset()}, which drop
what \code{ilm_future()} recorded, need \code{time} and \code{group}. A correlation over
time built from vectors has no columns of its own: it takes the ones \code{time}
and \code{group} name, or else the ones \code{\link[=ilm_future]{ilm_future()}} recorded, and with
neither, the prediction stops.
}

\section{Covariates given as draws}{

A covariate that is not known in advance, such as next month's
temperature, can be given as draws from its own forecast. \code{newdata} then
carries a \code{.draw} column: one block of rows per covariate draw, every block
listing the same rows in the same order, with only the drawn covariates
differing. Parameter draw s uses covariate draw ((s - 1) mod C) + 1 of the
C, so the prediction mixes over both. \code{nsim} is rounded up to a multiple of
C, with a message, so that each covariate draw is used equally often.

The prediction has one row per row of a block. A drawn covariate shows as
\code{NA} in the rows where it varies, \code{meta} records C and the drawn columns,
and a drawn row's estimate is its predictive median, as a new unit's is.
Intervals conditional on a covariate's point forecast leave out the
uncertainty in forecasting it (fpp3, section 10.3). In limn's own study
(\code{studies/findings/covariate_draws.md}) they covered too little: 0.75 to 0.83
for 90\% gaussian intervals, against about 0.90 with the covariate given as
draws.
\code{\link[=ilm_covariate_paths]{ilm_covariate_paths()}} builds the blocks from an illume model of the
covariate, and \code{\link[=ilm_as_draws]{ilm_as_draws()}} from paths simulated by another tool.
}

\section{Uncertainty}{

\itemize{
\item \code{"joint"} (the default): every parameter is drawn jointly, the variances
included. In limn's prototype study of few groups (studies/prototypes/), it
came closest to nominal coverage for a new unit's observation, though still
below it: 0.88 for 90\% intervals with 8 groups, against 0.85-0.87 for the
other methods it tried, each with a simulation standard error of about
0.01.
\item \code{"fixed"}: the variance parameters are held at their estimates, and
everything else is drawn.
\item \code{"latent"}: every parameter is held at its estimate, the dispersion
included, and only the random effects and the cells of a correlation over
time are drawn, from their distribution given the parameters. For a
gaussian model with a correlation over time this is a Kalman filter's
forecast, which treats the parameters as known; limn's tests check it
against \code{stats::KalmanForecast()}. ARIMA prediction intervals likewise
leave out the variation in the parameter estimates (fpp3, section 9.8).
\item \code{"none"}: the plug-in prediction at the estimates, with no parameter
uncertainty. A new unit's effects, and a series' cells at times it was not
fitted at (a forecast, a bridge or a backcast), are still drawn, and
covariates given as draws are still mixed over; otherwise a new
observation's distribution is the family's alone.
}
}

\section{Checks}{

What each check means and what to do about it, in the words it is printed with. A reference supports what was found and why; a remedy draws on it only where it says so, and anything marked as limn's own reasoning is not the source's:
\itemize{
\item \strong{few groups, means} (warn): a random term has k levels, fewer than about 20. With so few groups the fit's normal approximation understates the uncertainty in the fixed effects, since it takes no account of the uncertainty in the variances (the reference shows this for linear mixed models), so intervals for means cover as z-intervals do: in limn's coverage study with 8 groups, a 90\% interval for a typical unit's mean covered 0.84 to 0.86 for gaussian, Poisson, negative binomial and beta outcomes (binary outcomes covered 0.92). Reference: Kenward and Roger (1997), Biometrics 53, 983-997, pp. 983-984. No remedy for these intervals has been measured yet, so read them as somewhat too narrow; more groups is the sure cure.
\item \strong{few groups, new units} (warn): a random term has k levels, fewer than about 20. With so few groups the variance between them is poorly determined: the reference found its standard error too small with 50 groups or fewer, for linear models fitted by restricted maximum likelihood. In limn's coverage study with 8 groups, intervals for a new unit's observation covered 0.87 to 0.90 at 90\%. Reference: Maas and Hox (2005), Methodology 1, 86-92, pp. 86 and 89-90. Refit with reml = TRUE in illume::ilm_model(): restricted maximum likelihood is almost always at least as good as maximum likelihood, and sometimes better, especially at estimating variances (the reference, p. 89, citing Browne 1998). In limn's prototype study of few groups (studies/prototypes/) it raised 8-group coverage from 0.872 to 0.892 (Poisson) and from 0.891 to 0.903 (gaussian).
\item \strong{few groups, new units, REML fit} (warn): a random term has k levels, fewer than about 20. With so few groups the variance between them is poorly determined: the reference found its standard error too small with 50 groups or fewer, for linear models fitted by restricted maximum likelihood. In limn's coverage study with 8 groups, intervals for a new unit's observation covered 0.87 to 0.90 at 90\%. Reference: Maas and Hox (2005), Methodology 1, 86-92, pp. 86 and 89-90. The fit already used restricted maximum likelihood (REML), the refit this check otherwise suggests. In limn's prototype study of few groups (studies/prototypes/), with REML and 8 groups of 6, 90\% intervals for a new unit's observation covered 0.892 (Poisson) and 0.903 (gaussian), with simulation standard errors of 0.004 to 0.005. More groups is the sure cure.
\item \strong{unseen levels} (note): Some of the rows name a level the fit has not seen. Under groups = "fitted" a unit with no estimated effects can only be predicted as a new one. They are predicted as new units, with effects drawn from the fitted distribution, and marked in the new_level column. If they were meant to be units in the fit, check how their levels are spelt.
\item \strong{deterministic trend} (warn): The model trends in \code{time} (its trend terms). Forecasts past the data assume that the trend's slope does not change (the reference), so their intervals leave out any change in it (limn's own reasoning). A random slope in time gives each unit a trend of its own, and forecasts assume that each unit's slope does not change either (limn's own reasoning). Reference: Hyndman and Athanasopoulos (2021), Forecasting: Principles and Practice, 3rd edition, section 10.4. For longer horizons a stochastic trend is safer (the reference): a random walk, ar = ilm_rw1(~ time | series), lets the level move, and the intervals widen with the horizon.
\item \strong{boundary draws} (fail): Some of the draws of a variance are not finite. Its estimate (SD near zero) is at the edge of its range, where the data cannot resolve it, and the fit did not hold it there. Predict with uncertainty = "fixed", which holds the variances at their estimates, or refit with boundary = "avoid" in illume::ilm_model(), which keeps the variance off the edge with a small penalty.
\item \strong{draws not computed} (fail): Some of the draws of the mean could not be computed. Averaging over units failed where a draw's variance reaches a large value, as happens when the variance is poorly determined, for instance with few groups. Predict with uncertainty = "fixed", which holds the variances at their estimates.
\item \strong{draws not computed} (fail): Some of the draws of the mean could not be computed. A draw's value overflowed or was undefined. Predict with uncertainty = "fixed", which holds the variances at their estimates.
\item \strong{draws not computed} (fail): Some of the draws of a new observation's distribution could not be computed. In those draws the linear predictor or the dispersion ran so far out that the family overflowed. That happens when a parameter sits at the edge of its range (a variance or residual SD near zero, a correlation near 1) and the fit did not hold it there, so its draws spread without limit. Predict with uncertainty = "fixed", which holds the variances at their estimates, or uncertainty = "latent", which holds the dispersion too.
}
}

\section{What is not here yet}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Multinomial outcomes} come later, with forecasts of their latent
state.
\item \strong{Binomial outcomes with more than one trial} are predicted per trial.
}
}

\section{The prediction object}{

Read a prediction through \link[=as.data.frame.ilm_pred]{as.data.frame()} or
\link[=summary.ilm_pred]{summary()} where you can. Its elements are listed here so
that nothing in it is hidden. Every prediction, class \code{"ilm_pred"}, has:
\itemize{
\item \code{kind}: \code{"response"} for a new observation's distribution, held as the
family's parameters in each draw; \code{"mean"} or \code{"link"} for draws of the
mean or the linear predictor; or \code{"sample"} for simulated outcomes, which
is how \code{\link[=ilm_aggregate]{ilm_aggregate()}} holds some totals.
\item \code{rows}: a data frame with one row per predicted row: \code{newdata}'s columns
(one block's, with drawn covariates \code{NA}; see \emph{Covariates given as draws}),
and \code{new_level}, \code{TRUE} where a row names a level the fit has not seen.
A total or an average has its \code{by} columns and \code{n_rows} instead, and an
average also \code{weight}.
\item \code{weights}: the draws' weights, which sum to 1.
\item \code{meta}: a list recording how the prediction was made. It has \code{target},
\code{groups} (one \code{"term = choice"} per term), \code{uncertainty}, \code{draws} (the
number of parameter draws), \code{nsim}, \code{family}, \code{seed}, and \code{response}, the
name of the response column. With covariates given as draws it also has
\code{covariate_draws} and \code{drawn}. Where its time and series columns are known
(see \emph{Time and series columns}), it has \code{time} and \code{series}, their names.
\item \code{checks}: a data frame of the checks the prediction raised (see \emph{Checks}),
one row each, with columns \code{id}, \code{check}, \code{status}, \code{detail}, \code{cause},
\code{suggestion}, \code{reference} and \code{values}. It has no rows when nothing was
found.
\item \code{origin}: each row's forecast origin, its series' last observed time, or
\code{NA} for a row that is not ahead of its series' data.
}

A prediction straight from \code{ilm_predict()} or \code{\link[=ilm_forecast]{ilm_forecast()}} also has
\code{new_unit}, \code{TRUE} where a row's effects are drawn as a new unit's or a new
series'. A total or an average has \code{what} instead, saying how it was taken,
and \code{meta} records the same in \code{aggregate}.

What else it holds depends on \code{kind}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"response"}: \code{dist}, the family's distribution functions as
\code{illume::ilm_dist()} gives them; \code{lp}, a named list of rows-by-draws
matrices of linear predictors; \code{par}, the family's other parameters in
each draw; and \code{pvec}, any parameter that is a vector in each draw, such as
an ordered outcome's thresholds.
\item \code{"mean"} and \code{"link"}: \code{draws}, a rows-by-draws matrix of the predicted
values, and \code{estimate}, the value at the estimates (a new unit's or a
drawn row's predictive median; see \emph{Value}).
\item \code{"sample"}: \code{draws}, a rows-by-samples matrix of simulated outcomes;
\code{estimate}, their median; and \code{dist}, the family and whether it is
discrete.
}
}

\examples{
set.seed(1)
d <- data.frame(site = factor(rep(1:24, each = 5)), x = rnorm(120))
d$y <- rpois(120, exp(0.5 + 0.4 * d$x + rnorm(24, 0, 0.5)[d$site]))
fit <- illume::ilm_model(y ~ x + (1 | site), data = d, family = "poisson",
                         verbose = FALSE)
nd <- data.frame(site = c("1", "2", "new"), x = 0)
as.data.frame(ilm_predict(fit, nd, groups = "fitted", nsim = 200))
as.data.frame(ilm_predict(fit, nd, target = "response", groups = "new",
                          nsim = 200))
}
\references{
Hyndman, R. J. and Athanasopoulos, G. (2021). \emph{Forecasting:
Principles and Practice}, 3rd edition. OTexts. Section 9.8, "Forecasting"
(ARIMA), and section 10.3, "Forecasting" (dynamic regression) (online
edition, \url{https://otexts.com/fpp3/}, read 17 October 2026).
}
